# Plan a visibility tour over several targets (global_plan step 4, LocalVisTSP).
#
# WHAT vs HOW. This request says WHAT to solve. HOW to solve it -- which
# insertion rules run, whether 2-opt runs, the coverage k, and the base-locality
# radius -- comes from the planner/vistsp/* parameters, which the node re-reads
# on every call. That split matches the rest of the node, where every knob is a
# parameter, and it avoids ROS messages' lack of optional fields: an unset bool
# would arrive as false and silently disable an algorithm.
#
# LOCAL OR GLOBAL. planner/vistsp/base_locality_radius <= 0 leaves the base
# unconstrained, which runs the same pipeline over the whole environment. That
# is the intended baseline (VisCfgTSP), not a misconfiguration.

VisibilityTarget[] targets      # one ball per target; must not be empty
float64[] start_joints          # empty = use the robot's current state
string planner_type             # "VisRRT" or "VisPRM"; empty = planner/mode
---
bool success

# The best tour, expanded and (if shortcutting is on) smoothed per leg. Starts
# at the start configuration and returns to it, since the tour is a cycle.
trajectory_msgs/JointTrajectory trajectory

# Index in trajectory.points of each tour stop, in visiting order -- the leg
# boundaries. Two equal consecutive values mean one configuration serves
# several targets, so no motion happens between those stops.
int32[] stop_indices

# stop_target[i] is the target that stop i observes, or -1 for the start
# configuration. Same length as stop_indices. This is what lets a client draw
# which configuration is watching which target.
int32[] stop_target

# One entry per insertion rule that ran, in a fixed order, so two runs compare
# line by line. construction_costs is the tour before 2-opt and improved_costs
# after it; they are equal where 2-opt was off or found nothing. `best` indexes
# these arrays.
string[] algorithm_names
float64[] construction_costs
float64[] improved_costs
int32 best

# Wall-clock breakdown in seconds. Reported per phase because a single total
# would be almost entirely the coverage query and would hide everything the tour
# layer does. instance_seconds is where the metric closure runs its one Dijkstra
# per distinct goal vertex and is expected to dominate gtsp_seconds by orders of
# magnitude; these fields are what test that rather than assuming it. Populated
# even on failure, up to the phase that failed.
float64 coverage_seconds
float64 instance_seconds
float64 gtsp_seconds
float64 expand_seconds
float64 total_seconds

# True when the coverage query did not reach k configurations for every target.
# NOT a failure: a tour needs only one reachable configuration per target, so
# the tour above may still be perfectly good. A target with none at all makes
# the whole call fail instead.
bool coverage_incomplete
